; THE NUMBER-RENDERING NEED. Concrete laws at concrete numbers, deliberately:
; "renders 0 as the codepoint for '0'" is a statement a consumer can write
; without knowing anything about the implementation, and it is exactly what the
; consumer depends on. 48/55/49 are the codepoints for '0', '7' and '1'.
;
; These laws are all satisfied at NON-NEGATIVE inputs, which is all this
; consumer ever supplies (its indices start at 1). They deliberately say nothing
; about negatives — so a query answer that names more than one candidate is
; correct, not a defect, and the choice between them is the consumer's.
(defn wanted [] [(n Int)] Str
  SNil

  (prop zero-renders-as-zero []
    (== (wanted 0) (SCons 48 SNil)))
  (prop seven-renders-as-seven []
    (== (wanted 7) (SCons 55 SNil)))
  (prop ten-renders-as-two-digits []
    (== (wanted 10) (SCons 49 (SCons 48 SNil))))
  (prop single-digits-are-one-wide [(n Int)]
    (if (and (<= 0 n) (< n 10)) (== (str-len (wanted n)) 1) true)))
